Sanity check of the power arithmetic: 'n>=200 gives +-0.066 at 95% for p~0.5'
(plan.md section 6) is the SINGLE-PROPORTION Wald half-width. It is NOT the
resolution of an ARM-vs-ARM comparison; for that the paired (McNemar) design applies.

McNemar power.  Two formulations are reported because they disagree, and the
disagreement is itself the finding:
  (a) EXACT conditional sign test: condition on the observed number of discordant
      pairs m and ask whether X ~ Bin(m, 1/2).  This is what 'exact McNemar' means,
      and it is CONSERVATIVE - for every m below the point where 2*P(X<=k) <= 0.05
      has a solution the conditional rejection probability is 0, so power stays at
      alpha however large delta is.  With delta=0.05 you need m ~ 85 discordant
      pairs for the test to bite at all (see the self-checks below).
  (b) UNCONDITIONAL normal approximation on the discordant counts: the standard
      design-stage formula, which is what a sample-size argument must use.
  [self-check] n=200 q=0.10: exact |X-Y| threshold = 10 (normal approx z*SD = 8.5, SD = 4.36)
  [self-check] n=200 q=0.20: exact |X-Y| threshold = 13 (normal approx z*SD = 11.8, SD = 6.00)
  [self-check] n=400 q=0.10: exact |X-Y| threshold = 13 (normal approx z*SD = 12.1, SD = 6.16)
  [self-check] m=100: exact sign test rejects for X <= 39 or X >= 61; P(reject|H0) = 0.0352
  [self-check] m=200: exact sign test rejects for X <= 85 or X >= 115; P(reject|H0) = 0.0400
  [self-check] m=600: exact sign test rejects for X <= 275 or X >= 325; P(reject|H0) = 0.0454

